From: No Taxes through No Government <schirado@lab.cc.wmich.edu>
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 1994 00:03:35 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-9-359] REVIEW: "Genius: The Life and Science of Richard Feynman"

[This review was inspired by derek@alcor.org's comments regarding learning
unfamiliar material by reading the Extropians list, which I found myself
agreeing with quite strongly. I speak as a dropout and blue collar worker
(but a voracious reader) when I say that I find the list to be worth the
subscription fee many times over in sheer terms of encouraging personal
dynamic optimism. I hope I can inspire more lurkers out there to dive into
the ocean of learning, and this book is just the thing to encourage it.

PS: "Yo! Carl Feynman!" :]


_Genius: The Life and Science of Richard Feynman_, by James Gleick
ISBN 0-679-74704-4

James Gleick, the cover informed me, also wrote CHAOS, a highly recommended
book on the Extropians literature list. And a brief few skims convinced me
to plunk down my hard earned cash for _Genius: The Life and Science of
Richard Feynman_. Meticulously researched and simultaneously a can't-put-
it-down page turner, it serves another dual purpose by serving as both a
conventional (if unconventionally well-done) biography, and a rich portrait
of the human-inside-the-legend that, like Nathaniel Branden's _Judgment Day_,
does justice to the truth without diminishing our admiration for the people
in question, and leaves us, in fact, fonder of them for the experience.

"Genius" has always been a commonly badly applied term, and Gleick presents
us with a concise historical background of such. The consensus among the top
scientists, if there can be said to be such, is even more mysterious-sounding
to the laymen, especially coming from scientists: "Magic." Or darn close to
it, anyway. Gleick quotes an 1851 tract titled "Genius and Industry":

    "...at last, a question was proposed to the poor clerk -- a question
which weeks had been required to solve. Upon a simple piece of peper it was
answered immediately. 'But how', said the Professor, 'do you work this? show
me the rule!...The answer is correct but you have reached it by a different
way.'
    "'I have worked it,' said the clerk, 'from a rule in my own mind, I
cannot show you the law -- I never saw it myself; the law is in my mind.'
    "'Ah!' said the Professor, 'if you talk of a law within your mind, I have
done; I cannot follow you there.'"

Gleick mentions Thomas Kuhn's _Structure of Scientific Revolutions_ wherein
Kuhn distinguishes between normal science (problem solving, fleshing out of
existing frameworks, basically good solid craftsmanship) and revolutions
("intellectual upheavals through which knowledge lurches genuinely forward").
And he reminds us that the search for creative novelty is also nothing new:

    *Would I had phrases that are not known, utterances that are
     strange, in new language that has not been used, free from
     repetition, not an utterance which has grown stale, which men
     of old have spoken.*  An Egyptian scribe fixed those words in
     stone at the very dawn of recorded utterance -- already jaded,
     a milennium before Homer...

And he quotes evolutionary theorist Stephen Jay Gould on the seeming lack of
truly great intelligences of the present than we remember from our past:
"Giants have not ceded to mere mortals; rather, the boundaries...have been
restricted and the edges smoothed." For example, the .400 batter of baseball
lore has vanished despite greater physical conditioning, technical skills and
the population pool of prospective players [alliteration alert], because
overall standards have risen: "Gould showed, by statistical analysis, that
the extinction of the .400 hitter was only the more visible side of a general
softening of extremes: the .100 hitter has faded as well. The best and worst
all come close to the average."

But Feynman's flame burns bright in the pages of this book, and will burn as
bright in the reader's mind.


Feynman's father Melville, having grown up as a salesman, distrusted "suits"
instinctively, and taught Richard how to spot fraud and deceit, while at the
same time encouraging his burning curiosity about the world and its workings,
and lending the always practical touch to his tought. Herbert Jehle, upon
seeing Feynman's handling of mathematics, "was struck by the unabashed
pragmatism...so different from Dirac's more detached, more aesthetic tone.
'You Americans!' he said. 'Always trying to find a use for something.'"


Everything is here: the Los Alamos years, the brilliant lectures at Caltech
as a teacher of "basic physics" that became the "red books", the three volume
_Feynman lectures on Physics_, which universities worldwide attempted to
adopt as textbooks, only to give up and turn to "more manageable and less
radical alternatives" (but the books continued to sell steadily a generation
later, unlike true textbooks). (I consider his first two meetings with Dirac
almost worth the price of the book alone.)


A genuine tidbit which I'm sure isn't widely known is that in many ways,
Feynman was the father of nanotechnology, and given the interest of the
list in general on this topic, I'll permit myself to quote at a bit more
length. In 1959, twenty years before there was even a name for the field,
Feynman envisioned

   ...that same pinhead [which had the Lord's Prayer written on it, he
  told the American Physical Society] could hold the twenty-four volumes
  of the Encyclopaedia Britannica, pictures and all, if [it] were reduced
  25,000 times in each direction. A modest reduction, considering that
  the barely visible dots making up a halftone engraving would still
  contain a thousand or so atoms. For writing and reading this tiny
  Britannica, he proposed engineering techniques within the limits of
  contemporary technology: reversing the lenses of an electron micro-
  scope...and focusing a beam of ions to a small spot. At this scale,
  the world's entire store of book knowledge could be carried about in
  a small pamphlet. But direct reduction would be crude, he continued.
  Telephones and computers had given rise to a new way of thinking about
  information, and in terms of raw information -- allowing six or seven
  bits per letter and a generous one hundred atoms per bit -- all the
  world's books could be written in a cube no larger than a speck of dust.
  His audience, unaccustomed to lectures of this kind [at the Society],
  was enthralled...

   ...he talked about DNA (fifty atoms per bit of information) and about
  the capacity of living organisms to build tiny machinery, not just for
  information storage but for manipulation and manufacturing. He talked
  about computers: given millions of times more power, they would not just
  calculate faster but would reveal qualitatively different abilities,
  such as the ability to make judgments. "There is nothing I can see in
  the physical laws that says the computer elements cannot be made
  enormously smaller than they are now." He talked about problems of
  lubrication, and he talked about the realm where quantum-mechanical
  laws would take over. He envisioned machines that would make smaller
  machines, each of which would make machines that were smaller still,
  "It doesn't cost anything for materials, you see. So I want to build a
  billion tiny factories, models of each other, which are manufacturing
  simultaneously, drilling holes, stamping parts, and so on."  He
  concluded by offering a pair of one-thousand-dollar prizes: one for
  the first microscope-readable book page shrunk 25,000 times in each
  direction, and one for the first operating electric motor no larger than
  a 1/64th inch cube.

He got the motor that November, but the book page had to wait until 1985.
That initial lecture inspired many budding nanotechnologists, although
Feynman himself only occasionally flirted with the field for many years
thereafter, being immersed alternately in discovering new paradigms and
catching up on "other things" he wanted to learn: safecracking, bongo playing
(sometimes with strange, two-handed polyrhythms like seven against six and
thirteen against twelve), or idly working out a nuclear thrust motor ("not
quite practical but still plausible enough to be seized by the government,
patented, and immediately buried under an official secrecy order").
(Cypherpunks take note: you will love reading of the letters he and his first
wife, Arline, sent back and forth under the scrutiny of government censors
during his work on Trinity.) But eventually, he did return, in a roundabout
sort of fashion:

   in later years...when his son, Carl, ended his flirtation with philosophy
  and took up computer science, Feynman, too, looked again at the field he
  had helped pioneer at Los Alamos...for several summers he worked with the
  founders of Thinking Machines Corporation, near MIT, creating a radical
  approach to parallel processing; he served as a high-class technician,
  applying differential equations to the circuit diagrams, and as an
  occasional wise man among the young entrepreneurs ("Forget all that 'local
  minima' stuff -- just say there's a bubble caught in the crystal and you
  have to shake it out"). And he began to produce maverick research at the
  intersection of computing and physics: on how small computers could be; on
  entropy and the uncertainty principle in computing; on simulating quantum
  physics and probabilistic behavior; and on the possibility of building a
  quantum-mechanical computer, with packets of spin waves roaming
  ballistically back and forth through the logic gates.


With all the hard science an advanced intellectual could demand, the book is
still eminently readable to the mythical man-in-the-street. Gleick, following
Feynman's lead, makes no compromises when explaining the nuts and bolts of
everything from simple arithmetic to quantum physics, making every subject
understandable and exciting to people of almost every intellectual level. If
you're looking to inject some dynamic optimism into your life, this book is
just the ticket.

**





